93.m:
[93].1) Now the portents:
[93].2) on [abnormal] rain;
[93].3) on apparitions;
[93].4) on the croaking of jackal-frogs;
[93].5) on a family in discord;
[93].6) on an earthquake;
[93].7) on an eclipse of the sun;
[93].8) and of the moon;
[93].9) on dawn failing to rise;
[93].10) on a dreadfully severe year;
[93].11) on suspicion of an unlucky star;
[93].12) on Brahmins bearing weapons;
[93].13) on deities dancing, jumping, laughing, singing;
[93].14) on the entangling of two plows;
[93].15) and of two ropes or two threads;
[93].16) on the merging of fires;
[93].17) on twin calves being born in a sacrificial offering;
[93].18) and on a mare, a jenny, or a woman [giving birth to twins];
[93].19) where cows yield blood;
[93].20) on an ox suckling a cow;
[93].21) on a cow suckling a cow;
[93].22) on foam in the sky;
[93].23) on an unusual movement of ants;
[93].24) on an unusual movement of blue flies;
[93].25) on an unusual movement of bees;
[93].26) on the unknown;
[93].27) on a fissure;
[93].28) on water bubbling up in a waterless place;
[93].29) on sesame seeds oozing oil spontaneously;
[93].30) on sacrificial oblations being touched [by impurities];
[93].31) on whirlwinds turning counter-clockwise;
[93].32) on a sacrificial post sprouting;
[93].33) on a meteor;
[93].34) on a comet smoking toward the Seven Rṣis;
[93].35) on constellations shooting repeatedly;
[93].36) on [a bird] with meat in its mouth falling down;
[93].37) on a glow where there is no fire;
[93].38) on fire hissing;
[93].39) on ghee, oil, and honey overflowing;
[93].40) on a domestic fire burning a hall;
[93].41) and on an accidental [fire];
[93].42) on a bamboo bursting;
[93].43) on a water-jar splitting at its base, or on a cooking-pot or a groats container [cracking].

97.m:
[97].1) Now, where a family becomes quarrelsome, they say that it is seized by Nirṛti.
[97].2) There one should offer:
[97].3) with the two [stanzas beginning], "Far off the hostility..." [AVŚ 8.2.12];
[97].4) having offered into the fire with, "And you, O Agni, are unassailable and beyond reproach; truly you are unassailable. Made with unassailable mind, being unassailable, you carry the oblation. Bestow medicine upon us. Svāhā";
[97].5) in that very place he should offer these oblations:
[97].6) having offered with, "Pushing far off the flesh-eating fire, I place a fence for your survival. May Indra and Agni, thriving by the sacred word, long-lived, make you highest. To Indra and Agni, svāhā";
[97].7) he should offer with this hymn [beginning], "Away let Nirṛti go...":
[97].8) "Away let Nirṛti go; let nothing of her remain here. Away from her I thrust the snares of the warrior, the hundred and one deaths. / Those hundred and one snares that are yours, O Death, to kill mortal man—all of them we sacrifice away by the craft of the sacrifice. / Let them go forth from here, the deaths born of Nirṛti; we drive them further away. What darkness there is, we put away; life-breath and light we take upon ourselves. / Those hundred, O Varuṇa, those thousand great sacrificial snares of yours that are spread out—from them let Varuṇa, Soma, Indra, and all the well-praising Maruts release us. / The shining Brahman has risen up to the intermediate space and the sky; the Brahman has driven away death with the immortal. The Brahman is the direct overseer of good deeds; let the Brahman strike away taint and darkness from us."
[97].9) "A chosen ox [is the fee]," this is the same.

102.m:
[102].1) Now, where the year becomes dreadful, there one should offer:
[102].2) he should say, "The year that comes fiercely, that shakes the creatures of Prajāpati—that satisfaction which the deities know, we fashion you for that. The mother of the hunter, golden-bellied, yellow—we fashion you as that. Whatever is terrible of yours, whatever is poison of yours, that we deposit in our haters, in so-and-so";
[102].3) having offered with, "With auspiciousness for us, O year, with peace, with long life, to the year, svāhā";
[102].4) he should offer with this hymn [beginning], "The years, O Agni..." [AVŚ 2.6.1].
[102].5) That is the expiation there.

141.m:
[141].1) Now we shall explain the rule for the study of the Veda.
[141].2) Having performed the commencement of study on the full moon day of Śrāvaṇa or on that of Prauṣṭhapada, they should recite for four and a half months.
[141].3) In this manner the meters [are studied].
[141].4) And there is non-shaving of body hair.
[141].5) "And having commenced study for half a month, they should fast for three days having suspended study; the beginning is declared on the full moon day of Śrāvaṇa, the suspension of study is declared on that of Pauṣa."
[141].6) Now we shall explain the cessations of study.
[141].7) It ceases during those [occasions] arising in the course of study.
[141].8) At an ancestral offering;
[141].9) At the three: rising from birth impurity, and vomiting, there is performance [of cessation];
[141].10) Or when the teacher has died, and of those whose origin is human;
[141].11) As at an ancestral offering, so indeed in their case;
[141].12) And having accepted any article belonging to an ancestral offering, except for grain and clarified butter, there is cessation of study;
[141].13) Animate and inanimate;
[141].14) While cleaning the teeth;
[141].15) At the touch of a razor;
[141].16) When the fires are brought forth;
[141].17) When lightning or thunder occurs at midnight;
[141].18) When it has ceased after raining seven times, until the morning meal;
[141].19) When it has rained;
[141].20) At a thunderclap from a clear sky;
[141].21) At an earthquake;
[141].22) At the eclipse of a luminary, even in the proper season, until the [corresponding] time;
[141].23) In uneven [conditions] there is no proceeding.
[141].24) Now we shall declare the measure: it is the same for lightning and meteors; in the dark fortnights of Mārgaśīrṣa, Pauṣa, and Māgha, there are three Aṣṭakā festivals;
[141].25) And on the new moon day;
[141].26) And the three days of non-study.
[141].27) "At a birth and at a death, a period of ten nights is prescribed; for a teacher there should be ten nights, and among all one's own kinsmen."
[141].28) But at a birth impurity, one should not recite alone; he should avoid the instructor for three nights;
[141].29) And the son and wife of the teacher;
[141].30) Now, a student, a fellow student, a minor teacher who has arrived, he should avoid for a day and a night;
[141].31) Likewise a fellow student and the king;
[141].32) An unseasonal divine portent [causes cessation] until the [same] time.
[141].33) "All thunderclaps and the like are remembered as having no particular seasonal timing; and whatever other divine portent there is, all should be treated like a thunderclap."
[141].34) "Study of the metrical hymns is remembered as being in season, ritual texts out of season; but whatever cessation of study occurs in season after the morning meal reaches until the evening twilight."
[141].35) The early night is entirely omitted;
[141].36) At night, when [the thunder] sounds and at lightning, he should not recite the remainder;
[141].37) "At sunset, in the presence of two [planets] and in the presence of three, there is cleverness; then having eaten for that much time, the early night and both twilights;"
[141].38) "In water, in a cemetery, in bed, near an accused person, and in uncultivated tracts; when a corpse is within [the settlement], on a carriage road, and in a village containing Cāṇḍālas;"
[141].39) "In a bad odor, within earshot of a Śūdra, at a brown owl's sound, in danger, at a scream, during a Vaidhṛti [yoga], and in cities;"
[141].40) "And with an unwashed garment with which sexual intercourse has been performed; lying down, with feet raised, or in the presence of and near the teacher's lap;"
[141].41) "Having ceased during a swift wind, resumption is declared; having ceased throughout the latter part of the night, resumption is not seen."
[141].42) The full moon of Pauṣa is the standard regarding clouds, if they recite out of season;
[141].43) Rain, lightning, or thunder fails;
[141].44) They should undertake standing and sitting, chastity, and eating bland food for three nights.
[141].45) That is the expiation there; that is the expiation there.
